{
  "id": "zero_sum.minimax.minimax_from_loomis",
  "title": "Minimax Theorem by Simplified Loomis Induction",
  "kind": "theorem",
  "status": "formalized",
  "href": "zero_sum/zero_sum_minimax_minimax_from_loomis.html",
  "primary_topic": "zero_sum",
  "topics": [
    "zero_sum",
    "zero_sum.minimax"
  ],
  "body_html": "<h1>Minimax Theorem by Simplified Loomis Induction</h1>\n<p><em>Statement.</em> For every finite matrix game $A : I \\times J \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$|I|, |J| \\ge 1$, the maxmin and minmax values coincide:\n$$\n  \\lambda_0 = \\sup_{x \\in \\Delta(I)} \\min_{j \\in J} \\sum_i x_i A(i,j)\n            = \\inf_{y \\in \\Delta(J)} \\max_{i \\in I} \\sum_j y_j A(i,j) = \\mu_0.\n$$</p>\n<p>This node records the <strong>simplified Loomis</strong> proof actually used in the Lean\nformalisation, which works <strong>directly</strong> on $A$ (no separate $B$) and induces on\n$n = |I| + |J|$.  The general (positive $B$) Loomis theorem is held in\n<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"zero_sum.minimax.loomis_theorem\" href=\"minimax/zero_sum_minimax_loomis_theorem.html\">Loomis Theorem</a> as future work.</p>",
  "proof_html": "<p>Strong induction on $n = |I| + |J|$, starting at $n = 2$.</p>\n<p><strong>Base case</strong> $|I| = |J| = 1$. Both $\\lambda_0$ and $\\mu_0$ collapse to the\nunique payoff entry, so they coincide.</p>\n<p><strong>Inductive step.</strong> Assume the claim for all smaller dimensions. By the weak\nduality established in <a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"zero_sum.lam_mu_existence\" href=\"zero_sum_lam_mu_existence.html\">Existence of Optimal Mixed Strategies (Loomis Foundations)</a> we already have\n$\\lambda_0 \\le \\mu_0$; suppose strictly $\\lambda_0 &lt; \\mu_0$ for contradiction.\nPick optimisers $x^\\ast, y^\\ast$ from the same node.</p>\n<p>A short averaging argument shows that strict inequality forces <em>either</em> some\ncolumn $j_0$ with $\\sum_i x^\\ast_i A(i, j_0) &gt; \\lambda_0$, <em>or</em> some row $i_0$\nwith $\\sum_j y^\\ast_j A(i_0, j) &lt; \\mu_0$. By symmetry we handle the column\ncase.</p>\n<p>The smaller matrix game $A'$ obtained by dropping column $j_0$ has total\ndimension $n - 1$, so the induction hypothesis gives $\\lambda_0(A') = \\mu_0(A')$.\nExtending column strategies on $J' = \\{j \\ne j_0\\}$ to $J$ by putting zero mass\non $j_0$ shows $\\mu_0(A) \\le \\mu_0(A')$, hence $\\lambda_0(A) &lt; \\lambda_0(A')$.\nLet $x'$ be the inductive optimiser on $A'$. Continuity (from\n<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"zero_sum.loomis_route_compactness\" href=\"zero_sum_loomis_route_compactness.html\">Compactness and Continuity Scaffold for the Loomis Route</a>) lets us pick a strict convex\ncombination $t \\cdot x^\\ast + (1-t) \\cdot x'$ that still beats $\\lambda_0(A)$\nat every column — including $j_0$ thanks to the open neighborhood around\n$x^\\ast$ where $x^\\ast \\cdot A(\\cdot, j_0) &gt; \\lambda_0$. This contradicts the\nmaximality of $\\lambda_0(A)$.</p>\n<p>The row case is symmetric. Hence $\\lambda_0 = \\mu_0$.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MFoGT, Chapter 2, Section 2.3, Thm. 2.3.1] Laraki, Renault, and Sorin, <em>Mathematical Foundations of Game Theory</em>, <code>references/MechDesign.pdf</code>. Simplified Loomis induction proof of the finite von Neumann minimax theorem.</li>\n</ul>",
